#[
    Interop Example
]#

#[
    Calling C, Ruby and Crystal from Dragonstone.

    There is two styles, the first is calling the ffi
    directly while the other is a re-design of how to
    us it with dragonstone syntax via `invoke`.

    The old style has been retired `with <bare-name>` 
    plus an `as { ... }`, because as more functionality 
    was built into it, it became error prone.
]#

# Style One; call the ffi directly.
# ffi.call_<lang>(<function>, [<args>])`
define example_one
    ffi.call_ruby("puts", ["Hello from Ruby!"])
    ffi.call_crystal("puts", ["Hello from Crystal!"])
    ffi.call_c("printf", ["Hello from C!\n"])
end

example_one

# Style Two; call using the `invoke` keyword.
# 
# `invoke <Language>` opens the block, `end` closes it.
# `with` will be retained within the new style which is
# how to import a file from the other language, with
# the `invoke` keyword being updated as well to be
# lowercase like every other keyword in dragonstone 
# but the language name stays capitalized 
# (ex: `invoke Ruby`). The `with` keyword is NOT `use` 
# but work similar, `with` names something a FOREIGN 
# toolchain resolves.
# 
# #! Work In Progress
# Right now `with` will NOT resolve a file yet, but its 
# built and will work as I move through the bootstrap. In
# the future I will be adding a delimitter as well to 
# write foreign code within a dragonstone file.
# 
# NOTE: The `{}` will NOT be used as this delimiter, using 
# it here as an example. But here's two examples of how its
# planned:
# 
#       define example
#           invoke Ruby {
#               puts "Hi!"
#           }
#       end
# 
#       define example
#           invoke Ruby {
#               require "interop_ruby"
#               
#               puts "Hi!"
#           }
#       end
# 
# In addition to this, which works now, if you just want
# to add a foreign source without writing the code out:
define new_example_two
    invoke Ruby
        with "./interop_ruby"
    end
end

#! This is commented out because it will throw an error.
# new_example_two
